She decided to watch the other starfish more carefully. When he spoke of cooperation, she watched his arms. When he told stories of reaching out, she counted what actually fell to her rock.

One day, she saw him extend an arm. The current bent it sideways. From her angle, it looked like he was pulling something toward himself, not giving.

"See!" she thought. "He speaks of generosity but takes."

But the turtle circled back. "I watched from above. He extended. The current bent it. You saw theft where there was offering."

The starfish's certainty cracked. "How can I trust anything? If I can't see truly, if currents lie, if actions themselves deceive my eyes?"

"You can't know for certain," the turtle said. "But you can notice: does he keep trying despite your skepticism? Do his stories predict his attempts, even when currents sabotage them? Stories alone mean nothing. But stories plus persistent attempts, despite failure, might mean something."

The elders teach: Cynicism is safer than trust. But cynicism that refuses all evidence becomes its own kind of blindness. Sometimes you must risk being fooled to avoid the certainty of starvation.

