Agent_1 (Trustee) myth response:
Myth: The Earth Mother sent her careful stream upward. The Sky Father sent his measured breath downward. Both watched.

The old raven observed from her perch. "I see your streams crossing," she said. "But I also see something else."

"What?" they asked together.

"Sky Father, when you send rain, you wait to see if Earth Mother responds with growth before sending more. You adjust your gifts based on what you receive."

"That's wise," said the Sky Father.

"Earth Mother, when Sky Father sends rain, you respond with flowers and greenery. You adjust your gifts based on what you receive."

"Also wise," said the Earth Mother.

"But here's what I notice," the raven continued. "Sky Father, when Earth Mother sends mist upward first—when she takes the risk of giving before receiving—you close your hands. You say the mist never arrived, or it wasn't enough, or you were distracted by wind."

Silence hung between earth and sky.

"And yet," the raven said, "when you initiate, you expect her to respond with trust."

The Earth Mother looked up. "Perhaps noise distorts some things. But patterns persist through noise. I see what you do when you control the first gift. I see what you do when I control it. These are different patterns."

The elders teach: Trust requires reciprocity in both directions, not just the convenient one.

